1
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:27,960
- Everybody, heads up! - Keep it clear, now.

2
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:29,420
Keep it clear!

3
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:32,340
Stand back!

4
00:01:36,590 --> 00:01:38,340
Bring it forward, come on!

5
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:45,680
Slow it down!

6
00:01:56,190 --> 00:01:58,530
- Pushing team, move in there. - Move in.

7
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:02,950
- On your ready. - I want Tasers on full charge.

8
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,370
- Steady! - Go on. Step back in.

9
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:09,370
Ready. Move it.

10
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:12,040
And push.

11
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,470
Locked. Loading team, step away.

12
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:22,800
Move it.

13
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,440
Jophery, raise the gate.

14
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:52,710
- Block the opening! - Don't let her get out!

15
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:03,600
Somebody help him!

16
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:05,680
Work her back!

17
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:18,530
Shoot her!

18
00:03:57,070 --> 00:04:00,400
I heard that Hammond's not even here. He sends his apologies.

19
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:03,570
There's a $20 million lawsuit by the family of that worker

20
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:06,240
and you're telling me that Hammond can't see me?

21
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:09,450
He had to leave early, his daughter's getting a divorce.

22
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:12,580
I understand, but we need to deal with the situation now.

23
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:14,830
The insurance company... You okay?

24
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:21,880
The underwriters feel that the accident's raised safety questions about the park.

25
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:24,550
That makes the investors very anxious.

26
00:04:24,630 --> 00:04:28,220
I had to promise to conduct a very thorough, on-site inspection.

27
00:04:29,810 --> 00:04:32,480
Hammond hates inspections. They slow everything down.

28
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:36,230
I need to or they'll pull the funding. That'll slow him down even more.

29
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:43,150
Watch your head.

30
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:48,580
If two experts sign off on the island, the insurance guys will back off.

31
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:52,120
I've already got Ian Malcolm, but they think he's too trendy.

32
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:53,790
They want Alan Grant. Grant?

33
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:56,120
You'll never get him out of Montana.

34
00:04:56,830 --> 00:04:58,130
Why not?

35
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,300
Why not?

36
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:05,630
Because Grant's like me.

37
00:05:07,510 --> 00:05:09,050
He's a digger.

38
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:56,520
Dr. Grant, Dr. Sattler, we're ready to try again.

39
00:05:57,810 --> 00:05:59,400
I hate computers.

40
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:01,360
The feeling's mutual.

41
00:06:16,330 --> 00:06:17,540
Take a look.

42
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:21,670
Did it work?

43
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:24,840
How long does this take? Should bring an immediate return.

44
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:29,010
You shoot the radar into the ground, and the bone bounces the image back.

45
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:31,180
Bounces it back...

46
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:35,310
This new program is incredible.

47
00:06:35,850 --> 00:06:39,350
A few more years' development, and we won't even have to dig anymore.

48
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:41,150
Where's the fun in that?

49
00:06:41,730 --> 00:06:45,230
It's a little distorted, but I don't think it's the computer.

50
00:06:46,230 --> 00:06:49,450
Post-mortem contraction of the posterior neck ligaments.

51
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:52,740
Velociraptor? Yes. Good shape, too.

52
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:56,240
It's five, six feet high. I'm guessing nine feet long.

53
00:06:56,330 --> 00:06:57,660
Look... What'd you do?

54
00:06:57,750 --> 00:06:59,080
He touched it.

55
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:03,130
Dr. Grant's not machine-compatible. Hell, they've got it in for me.

56
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:06,710
And look at the half-moon shaped bones in the wrists.

57
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,470
It's no wonder these guys learned how to fly.

58
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:12,640
No, seriously.

59
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:19,600
Maybe dinosaurs have more in common with present-day birds than with reptiles.

60
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:23,190
Look at the pubic bone, turned backward, just like a bird.

61
00:07:23,270 --> 00:07:27,570
Look at the vertebrae, full of air sacs and hollows, like a bird.

62
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:30,780
And even the word "Raptor" means "bird of prey."

63
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:33,160
That doesn't look very scary.

64
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:37,450
More like a 6-foot turkey.

65
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:41,290
A turkey?

66
00:07:44,210 --> 00:07:47,420
Imagine yourself in the Cretaceous period. Here we go.

67
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:51,680
You'd get your first look at this 6-foot turkey as you enter a clearing.

68
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:54,970
He moves like a bird, lightly bobbing his head.

69
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:59,470
You keep still because you think maybe his visual acuity is based on movement,

70
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,310
like T-Rex and he'll lose you if you don't move.

71
00:08:02,390 --> 00:08:04,520
But no, not Velociraptor.

72
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:08,940
You stare at him and he just stares right back.

73
00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:14,280
And that's when the attack comes. Not from the front, but from the side.

74
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:18,700
From the other two Raptors you didn't even know were there.

75
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:24,920
Because Velociraptor's a pack hunter. He uses coordinated attack patterns

76
00:08:25,130 --> 00:08:27,420
and he is out in force today.

77
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:30,920
And he slashes at you with this,

78
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:35,510
a 6-inch retractable claw, like a razor, on the middle toe.

79
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:39,640
He doesn't bother to bite your jugular like a lion. No.

80
00:08:40,010 --> 00:08:41,850
He slashes at you

81
00:08:42,430 --> 00:08:44,520
here or here...

82
00:08:44,810 --> 00:08:45,980
Alan.

83
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:50,320
Or maybe across the belly, spilling your intestines.

84
00:08:51,570 --> 00:08:56,570
The point is, you are alive when they start to eat you.

85
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:01,580
So, you know, try to show a little respect.

86
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:03,500
Okay.

87
00:09:11,210 --> 00:09:15,680
Alan, if you wanted to scare the kid, you could've pulled a gun on him.

88
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:18,260
Yeah, I know. Kids.

89
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:21,100
You want to have one of those?

90
00:09:21,260 --> 00:09:26,140
I don't want that kid, but a breed of the child could be intriguing.

91
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:28,900
I mean, what's so wrong with kids?

92
00:09:28,980 --> 00:09:33,400
They're noisy, they're messy, they're expensive.

93
00:09:33,490 --> 00:09:34,990
Cheap, cheap.

94
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:36,490
They smell.

95
00:09:36,570 --> 00:09:38,240
They don't smell! Some smell.

96
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:40,620
Give me a break! Babies smell.

97
00:09:48,210 --> 00:09:50,250
Cover the site! Cover up the dig!

98
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:52,920
Tell them to shut down! Cut the machine!

99
00:09:53,250 --> 00:09:55,590
- Cover it all up. - Pull it over.

100
00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:01,010
Just cut it, will you? Shut it down!

101
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:02,850
What?

102
00:10:17,450 --> 00:10:20,660
What the hell do you think you're doing in here?

103
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:22,870
We were saving that.

104
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:25,870
For today, I guarantee it.

105
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:30,460
Who in God's name do you think you are? John Hammond.

106
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:33,920
And I'm delighted to meet you finally in person, Dr. Grant.

107
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:39,970
So, I can see that my $50,000 a year has been well spent.

108
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:41,890
Who's the jerk?

109
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:45,390
This is our paleobotanist, Dr... Sattler.

110
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:47,770
Ellie, this is Mr. Hammond.

111
00:10:48,310 --> 00:10:51,230
I'm sorry about the dramatic entrance, Dr. Sattler, but...

112
00:10:51,310 --> 00:10:53,310
Did I say "jerk"? ...we're in a hurry.

113
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:56,820
Will you have a drink? We won't let it get warm. Sit down.

114
00:10:57,490 --> 00:11:00,660
- Let me have this. - I'll just get a glass or two. I can do it.

115
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:03,990
There's samples all over here. I know my way around the kitchen.

116
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:06,240
Now, I'll get right to the point.

117
00:11:07,830 --> 00:11:10,160
I like you. Both of you.

118
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:13,840
I can tell instantly about people. It's a gift.

119
00:11:17,090 --> 00:11:20,090
I own an island off the coast of Costa Rica.

120
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:24,180
I've leased it from the government and during the last five years,

121
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:26,760
I've been setting up a biological preserve.

122
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:29,230
Really spectacular. Spared no expense.

123
00:11:29,310 --> 00:11:32,770
Make the one I've got down in Kenya look like a petting zoo.

124
00:11:32,850 --> 00:11:37,030
And there's no doubt our attractions will drive kids out of their minds.

125
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:40,490
And what are those? Small versions of adults, honey.

126
00:11:40,570 --> 00:11:42,490
And not just kids, everyone.

127
00:11:42,570 --> 00:11:45,910
We're going to open next year, if the lawyers don't kill me first.

128
00:11:45,990 --> 00:11:48,490
I don't care for lawyers, do you?

129
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:51,920
I don't really know any. Don't really know any.

130
00:11:52,250 --> 00:11:57,090
I do, there's a particular pebble in my shoe, who represents my investors.

131
00:11:57,340 --> 00:12:00,170
Says that they insist on outside opinions.

132
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:01,880
What kind of opinions?

133
00:12:02,010 --> 00:12:05,010
Well, your kind, not to put too fine a point on it.

134
00:12:05,090 --> 00:12:09,180
Let's face it, in your particular fields, you're the top minds.

135
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:11,730
And if I could just persuade you

136
00:12:12,190 --> 00:12:15,360
to sign off on the park, give it your endorsement,

137
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:19,860
maybe even pen a wee testimonial, I could get back on schedule.

138
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:25,070
Why would they care what we think? What kind of park is this?

139
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:29,330
It's right up your alley.

140
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:35,420
Why don't you come down, just the pair of you, for the weekend?

141
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:38,670
I'd love to have an opinion of a paleobotanist as well.

142
00:12:39,170 --> 00:12:41,670
I've got a jet standing by at Choteau.

143
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:45,970
I'm sorry. This isn't possible.

144
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:48,430
We just dug up a new skeleton...

145
00:12:48,510 --> 00:12:52,600
I could compensate you by fully funding your dig...

146
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:55,690
This is a very unusual time. The timing is...

147
00:12:55,810 --> 00:12:58,150
...for a further three years.

148
00:13:03,150 --> 00:13:04,610
Where's the plane?

149
00:13:05,990 --> 00:13:07,120
Okay. Three years.

150
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:08,490
Cheers.

151
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:52,040
Dodgson!

152
00:14:00,210 --> 00:14:02,000
You shouldn't use my name.

153
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,840
Dodgson. We've got Dodgson here.

154
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:09,680
See, nobody cares. Nice hat.

155
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:13,100
What're you trying to look like, a secret agent?

156
00:14:13,430 --> 00:14:14,930
Well? $750,000.

157
00:14:15,520 --> 00:14:17,940
On delivery, $50,000 more for each viable embryo.

158
00:14:18,060 --> 00:14:21,110
That's $1 .5 million if you get all 15 species off the island.

159
00:14:21,190 --> 00:14:22,270
I'll get them all.

160
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:26,280
Remember, viable embryos. They're no use to us if they don't survive.

161
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,610
How am I supposed to transport them?

162
00:14:32,410 --> 00:14:34,120
The bottom screws open.

163
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:37,460
That's great. Oh, my God.

164
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:40,040
It's cooled and compartmentalized inside.

165
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:43,380
You guys. That's great. Customs can even check it if they want to.

166
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:44,960
Let me see. Go on.

167
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:48,930
There's enough coolant for 36 hours. No menthol?

168
00:14:49,130 --> 00:14:52,050
The embryos have to be back here in San Jos?by then.

169
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:53,890
That's up to your guy on the boat.

170
00:14:53,970 --> 00:14:57,640
7:00 tomorrow night on the East Dock. Make sure he gets it right.

171
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:01,940
How are you planning to beat security? I've got an 18-minute window.

172
00:15:02,110 --> 00:15:05,980
Eighteen minutes and your company catches up on 10 years of research.

173
00:15:10,110 --> 00:15:12,120
Don't get cheap on me, Dodgson.

174
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:16,660
That was Hammond's mistake.

175
00:15:30,340 --> 00:15:33,340
So you two dig up dinosaurs?

176
00:15:35,850 --> 00:15:37,770
Well... We try to.

177
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:46,690
You'll have to get used to Dr. Malcolm.

178
00:15:46,820 --> 00:15:51,200
He suffers from a deplorable excess of personality, especially for a mathematician.

179
00:15:51,280 --> 00:15:53,160
Chaotician. Chaotician, actually.

180
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:55,030
John doesn't subscribe to chaos,

181
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:57,620
particularly what it says about his science project.

182
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:01,410
Ian, you've never been able to sufficiently explain your concerns...

183
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:04,380
Because of the behavior of the system in phase space?

184
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:07,710
A load, if I may say so, of fashionable number crunching...

185
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:09,050
Don't.

186
00:16:09,170 --> 00:16:12,220
Dr. Sattler, Dr. Grant, you've heard of Chaos Theory?

187
00:16:12,510 --> 00:16:14,890
No. No? Non-linear equations?

188
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:17,720
Strange attractors?

189
00:16:19,850 --> 00:16:24,480
I refuse to believe that you aren't familiar with the concept of attraction.

190
00:16:28,570 --> 00:16:31,860
I bring scientists. You bring a rock star.

191
00:16:36,320 --> 00:16:37,410
There it is.

192
00:17:05,270 --> 00:17:10,020
Bad wind shears. We have to drop pretty fast. Hold on, this will be thrilling.

193
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:40,470
But you need that piece over here, and that piece...

194
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:43,310
We'll have landed by the time you get it right.

195
00:17:44,310 --> 00:17:45,520
Great.

196
00:19:03,850 --> 00:19:06,470
The full 50 miles of perimeter fence are in place?

197
00:19:06,560 --> 00:19:09,770
And the concrete moats, and the motion-sensor tracking systems.

198
00:19:09,890 --> 00:19:13,060
Donald, dear boy, relax. Try and enjoy yourself.

199
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,230
Let's get this straight. This is not a weekend excursion.

200
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:19,400
This is a serious investigation of the stability of the island.

201
00:19:19,530 --> 00:19:22,370
Your investors, whom I represent, are deeply concerned.

202
00:19:22,450 --> 00:19:25,950
Forty-eight hours from now, if they're not convinced, I'm not convinced.

203
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:27,790
I'll shut you down, John.

204
00:19:28,410 --> 00:19:32,420
In 48 hours, I'll be accepting your apologies.

205
00:19:41,930 --> 00:19:44,220
All right, slow down. Slow. Stop!

206
00:19:46,430 --> 00:19:48,100
This shouldn't be here.

207
00:20:07,950 --> 00:20:10,750
Alan, this species of Veriforman has been extinct

208
00:20:10,830 --> 00:20:13,580
since the Cretaceous Period. I mean, this thing...

209
00:20:13,670 --> 00:20:15,000
What?

210
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:45,950
Look at that.

211
00:20:49,330 --> 00:20:51,330
It's a dinosaur.

212
00:20:55,670 --> 00:20:59,000
You did it. Crazy son of a bitch, you did it.

213
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:04,010
We could tear up the rule book on cold-bloodedness. It doesn't apply.

214
00:21:04,090 --> 00:21:06,430
They're wrong. This is a warm-blooded creature.

215
00:21:06,510 --> 00:21:10,220
This thing doesn't live in a swamp. This thing's got a 25, 27-foot neck?

216
00:21:10,310 --> 00:21:12,850
The Brachiosaurus? 30. Thirty feet.

217
00:21:29,410 --> 00:21:32,040
We're gonna make a fortune with this place.

218
00:21:36,870 --> 00:21:38,380
How fast are they?

219
00:21:38,500 --> 00:21:41,710
Well, we clocked the T-Rex at 32 miles an hour.

220
00:21:43,550 --> 00:21:44,920
T-Rex?

221
00:21:46,050 --> 00:21:48,010
You said you've got a T-Rex?

222
00:21:49,050 --> 00:21:50,180
Say again.

223
00:21:51,350 --> 00:21:52,720
We have a T-Rex.

224
00:21:54,600 --> 00:21:56,770
Put your head between your knees.

225
00:21:57,230 --> 00:21:58,440
Dr. Grant,

226
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:00,860
my dear Dr. Sattler,

227
00:22:01,230 --> 00:22:04,440
welcome to Jurassic Park.

228
00:22:28,090 --> 00:22:29,840
They're moving in herds.

229
00:22:32,100 --> 00:22:33,680
They do move in herds.

230
00:22:41,770 --> 00:22:43,360
How did you do this?

231
00:22:46,150 --> 00:22:47,490
I'll show you.

232
00:23:11,890 --> 00:23:13,640
Good day.

233
00:23:14,310 --> 00:23:17,060
The most advanced amusement park in the entire world,

234
00:23:18,770 --> 00:23:20,940
incorporating all the latest technologies.

235
00:23:21,020 --> 00:23:24,320
And I'm not talking just about rides. Everybody has rides.

236
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:28,650
No, we've made living biological attractions so astounding

237
00:23:28,820 --> 00:23:32,320
that they'll capture the imagination of the entire planet.

238
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:34,490
So, what are you thinking?

239
00:23:35,740 --> 00:23:38,830
That we're out of a job. Don't you mean "extinct"?

240
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:50,760
Why don't you all sit down? Donald, sit down. Why don't you all sit down? Donald, sit down.

241
00:23:51,680 --> 00:23:54,720
Here he comes. Well, here I come.

242
00:23:59,390 --> 00:24:00,600
Hello.

243
00:24:01,390 --> 00:24:04,020
Say hello. Hello.

244
00:24:05,190 --> 00:24:06,360
Hello, John.

245
00:24:07,020 --> 00:24:08,820
Yes, I've got lines.

246
00:24:08,900 --> 00:24:12,360
Well, fine, I guess. But, how did I get here?

247
00:24:13,860 --> 00:24:17,370
Well, let me show you. First, I'll need a drop of blood.

248
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:19,200
Your blood.

249
00:24:19,290 --> 00:24:20,540
Right.

250
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:23,370
That hurt.

251
00:24:23,790 --> 00:24:26,880
- Relax, John. - It's all part of the miracle of cloning.

252
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:29,050
Hello, John.

253
00:24:31,050 --> 00:24:32,300
Cloned from what?

254
00:24:32,380 --> 00:24:35,090
Loy extractions never recreated an intact DNA strand.

255
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:37,260
Not without massive sequence gaps.

256
00:24:37,390 --> 00:24:39,260
Paleo-DNA, from what source?

257
00:24:39,930 --> 00:24:43,390
Where do you get 100 million-year-old dinosaur blood?

258
00:24:48,070 --> 00:24:49,400
What? What?

259
00:24:50,150 --> 00:24:53,110
Well, Mr. DNA, where did you come from?

260
00:24:53,280 --> 00:24:54,570
From your blood.

261
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:58,950
Just one drop of your blood contains billions of strands of DNA,

262
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:01,580
the building blocks of life.

263
00:25:02,290 --> 00:25:06,960
A DNA strand like me is a blueprint for building a living thing.

264
00:25:07,330 --> 00:25:12,300
And sometimes animals that went extinct millions of years ago, like dinosaurs,

265
00:25:12,670 --> 00:25:17,640
left their blueprints behind for us to find. We just had to know where to look.

266
00:25:17,800 --> 00:25:22,640
A hundred million years ago, there were mosquitoes just like today.

267
00:25:22,770 --> 00:25:26,100
And just like today, they fed on the blood of animals.

268
00:25:26,350 --> 00:25:30,940
Even dinosaurs. Sometimes, after biting a dinosaur,

269
00:25:31,280 --> 00:25:35,780
mosquitoes would land on the branch of a tree and get stuck in the sap.

270
00:25:36,110 --> 00:25:40,990
After a long time, the tree sap would get hard and become fossilized,

271
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:46,000
just like a dinosaur bone, preserving the mosquito inside.

272
00:25:46,710 --> 00:25:50,290
This fossilised tree sap, which we call amber,

273
00:25:50,630 --> 00:25:54,880
waited for millions of years with the mosquito inside

274
00:25:55,050 --> 00:25:58,050
until Jurassic Park scientists came along.

275
00:25:58,970 --> 00:26:03,270
Using sophisticated techniques, they extract the preserved blood

276
00:26:03,350 --> 00:26:07,690
from the mosquito, and bingo: dino DNA!

277
00:26:08,730 --> 00:26:13,030
A full DNA strand contains three billion genetic codes.

278
00:26:13,650 --> 00:26:17,740
If we looked at screens like these once a second for eight hours a day,

279
00:26:17,820 --> 00:26:21,990
it'd take two years to look at the entire DNA strand.

280
00:26:22,080 --> 00:26:26,750
It's that long. Since it's so old, it's full of holes.

281
00:26:27,370 --> 00:26:30,040
Now that's where our geneticists take over.

282
00:26:30,580 --> 00:26:34,210
Thinking machine super-computers and gene sequencers

283
00:26:34,300 --> 00:26:36,470
break down the strand in minutes.

284
00:26:36,550 --> 00:26:38,590
And virtual-reality displays

285
00:26:38,680 --> 00:26:41,850
show our geneticists the gaps in the DNA sequence.

286
00:26:42,430 --> 00:26:44,930
We used the complete DNA of a frog

287
00:26:45,020 --> 00:26:48,940
to fill in the holes and complete the code.

288
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:53,690
Whew! And now, we can make a baby dinosaur.

289
00:26:56,860 --> 00:27:00,910
This score is only temporary. It all has very dramatic music, of course.

290
00:27:01,530 --> 00:27:04,080
A march or something. It hasn't been written yet.

291
00:27:04,200 --> 00:27:06,080
And then, the tour moves on.

292
00:27:15,550 --> 00:27:20,340
- Well, look here. - Those hard-working cowpokes you see behind us...

293
00:27:20,430 --> 00:27:22,800
This is overwhelming, John.

294
00:27:22,970 --> 00:27:27,140
Are these characters autoerotica?

295
00:27:27,220 --> 00:27:29,890
No, we have no animatronics here.

296
00:27:30,020 --> 00:27:33,360
Those people are the real miracle workers of Jurassic Park.

297
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:36,650
...in unfertilized emu or ostrich eggs.

298
00:27:37,440 --> 00:27:40,400
Wait a minute. How do you interrupt the cellular mitosis?

299
00:27:40,490 --> 00:27:43,200
Can't we see the unfertilized eggs? Shortly.

300
00:27:44,320 --> 00:27:48,250
Now a whole team of genetic engineers goes to work on...

301
00:27:48,330 --> 00:27:51,710
Can't you stop these things? I'm sorry. It's kind of a ride.

